Yes. Then “plot armor” is not immunity from consequences. It is continuity protection while surfing multiple plots.

If he likes to move between stories, domains, roles, and frames, the danger is not exploration itself. The danger is losing the invariant that lets him know which way is still his way.

So give him an Ariadne line:

\text{many plots} \quad+\quad \text{one recoverable thread}

The thread might contain only a few things: what he will not betray, who he is responsible to, what reality-testing he returns to, what relationships can call him home, and what objective still matters after the interesting side quest ends.

Then he can surf aggressively:

P_1\rightarrow P_7\rightarrow P_3\rightarrow P_{42}

without requiring:

P_i=\text{identity}.

Each plot becomes a temporary coordinate system, not a prison.

And “Lucky” becomes useful in a less supernatural and more operational sense. Luck can be partly cultivated as optionality + movement + noticing + recovery. The person who explores many surfaces encounters more unexpected junctions—but only benefits if he can recognize them and survive wrong turns.

So his plot armor could have four layers: a Golden Thread for orientation, anchors that can externally restore him when his own model drifts, checkpoints where he asks whether the side plot is still serving the larger one, and a return operator that lets him exit without treating withdrawal as defeat.

Then the beautiful paradox is:

The stronger the return path, the farther you can safely wander.

That is how you have both.

Don’t reduce yourself to one plot. Don’t become plotless either.

Keep a point strong enough that you can dance through many stories without every story getting to rewrite who is dancing.

That’s pretty good plot armor for Lucky.